  <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <div type="mūlam" rend="parallel">
      <lg xml:id="TNV12">
      <l>vēṅkai kāntaṭ cīya mayilē</l>
<l>pāṅkar marāmarañ cennāy paruntē</l>
<l>kuruntē mullai yiralai puṟavē</l>
<l>kāñci kaḻunīr mēti yaṉṉaṅ</l>
<l>kaitai muṇṭakaṅ karāmē kampuḷeṉ</l>
<l>ṟeytiya piṟavu miyampiya vilaṅkē.</l></lg>
<lg xml:lang="en">
  <l>The kino tree, the Malabar glory lily, the lion, and the peacock;</l>
<l>the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">pāṅkar</hi></term> tree, the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">marā</hi></term> tree, the wild dog,<note xml:lang="en" place="foot"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cennāy</hi></q> is treated here as a lexicalised item, though one can break it down into <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cem</hi></term> (‘red’) + <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">nāy</hi></term> (‘dog’). The precise identification of this is not clear. It might refer to either a jackal or another type of wild dog, possibly a dhole. Both are reddish in colour.</note> and the kite;</l>
<l>the wild-lime tree, jasmine, the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">iralai</hi></term> deer, and the dove;</l>
<l>the portia tree, the water lily, the buffalo, and the swan;</l>
<l>the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kaitai</hi></term> tree, the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">muṇṭakam</hi></term> tree, the alligator, and the waterfowl</l>
<l> – [these] and appropriate others [are] the living things mentioned.</l>
</lg>
</div>
</body>
</text>
<text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <head xml:lang="en">Commentary</head>
      <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
      <p xml:id="TNV12comm1">eṉpatu vilaṅkām āṟuṇarttutaṉutaliṟṟu.</p>
      <p xml:lang="en">The quoted [<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>] intends to make known the way in which the living things occur.</p>
    </div>
    <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
      <p xml:id="TNV12comm2">vēṅkai maramum pūṅ kulaik kāntaḷuñ cīyamāvumayiṟ puḷḷum ōmai maramumarām pūvuñ cennāy māvum 
paruntup puḷḷuṅ kurunta maramuṅ koṭip pū mullaiyum iralai māvum puṟavup puḷḷuṅ 
kāñci maramum pūṅ kaḻunīrum erumai māvum aṉṉap puḷḷun tāḻai maramum pūṅ kaḻi muḷḷiyumutalai māvuṅ 
kampuṭ puḷḷum piṟavum ain tiṇaikkuriya vilaṅkām eṉṟa vāṟu.</p>
<p xml:lang="en">The kino tree, clusters of Malabar glory lilies, the lion, and the peacock; 
  the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">ōmai</hi></term> tree, the 
  <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">marām</hi></term> flower, the wild dog, and the kite; 
  the wild-lime tree, the creeper jasmine, the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">iralai</hi></term> deer, 
  and the dove; the portia tree, the purple water lily, the 
  buffalo, and the swan; the screwpine tree, the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">muḷḷi</hi></term> plant 
  from the backwaters, the crocodile, and the waterfowl – [these] and others are the living things appropriate for the five poetic settings.</p>
</div>
<div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
  <p xml:id="TNV12comm3">piṟavum eṉṟataṉāl akiluñ cantaṉamum vēṅkaip pūvuñ cuṉaik kuvaḷaip pūvum 
puliyum yāṉaiyuṅ kiḷiyum vēṅkaiyuṅ<anchor xml:id="venkai"/> kurām pūvum pātiram pūvumurukkuṅ<anchor xml:id="murukku"/> koṉṟaiyun tōṉṟip pūvumuyaluṅ 
kāṉaṅ kōḻiyu<anchor xml:id="koli"/>marutum vañciyun tāmaraip pūvunīrnāyun tārāvum<anchor xml:id="tara"/> puṉṉaiyuñāḻaluneytaṟ pūvum 
aṉṉamumakaṉṟilum<anchor xml:id="akanril"/> uḷ ḷiṭṭaṉa vellāṅ koḷka.</p>
<p xml:lang="en">By <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">piṟavum</hi></q>, understand that all [of these] are included: 
the eagle-wood tree, the sandalwood tree, the kino tree flower, the mountain-pool water lily, the panther, the elephant, 
the parakeet, and the tiger;<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">puli</hi></q> and 
  <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">vēṅkai</hi></q> both normally refer to a tiger. Here one of them 
  presumably refers to another big cat, though it is not clear which one.</note> the bottle-flower, the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">pātiram</hi></term> flower,
 and the <term xml:lang="sa-Latn"><hi rend="italic">palaś</hi></term> tree; the laburnum tree, the Malabar glory lily, the hare, 
and the forest fowl; the myrobalan tree, the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">vañci</hi></term> tree, the lotus flower, the otter, and the duck; 
the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">puṉṉai</hi></term> tree, the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">ñāḻal</hi></term> tree, the blue water lily, the swan, and the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">makaṉṟil</hi></term> bird.</p>
</div>
</body>
</text>
